#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:59
msgid ""
"The goal of each solver is the solve the problem as fast as possible while "
"maintaining a certain level of accuracy. The accuracy is controlled by the "
"tolerances, one for the relative residual and one for the absolute residual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:61
msgid ""
"At each iteration, the solver computes an estimate of the current error :"
"math:`\\mathbf{e_k} = \\mathbf{y_k} - \\mathbf{y}`, where :math:"
"`\\mathbf{y_k}` is the current approximation and :math:`\\mathbf{y}` is the "
"exact solution. It accepts the current approximation if the error is small "
"enough according to the set relative tolerance :math:`\\epsilon_r` and "
"absolute tolerance :math:`\\epsilon_a`, using the following criteria:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:63
msgid ""
"\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( \\frac{e_{k,i}}{\\epsilon_r |y_i| "
"+ \\epsilon_a} \\right)^2 } \\leq 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:68
msgid ""
"The relative tolerance is used to control the error relative to the current "
"magnitude of the solution, while the absolute tolerance is used to control "
"the error in absolute terms (i.e. when the solution is close to zero). Note "
"that these errors are computed in relation to the solution for the state "
"variables of the equations, and not the output variables that might be "
"computed by PyBaMM after the solution is found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:71
msgid "Default tolerances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:73
msgid ""
"The default tolerances for the solvers are always given in the documentation "
"of each solver. For example, the default tolerances for the IDAKLU solver "
"can be seen `here <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/stable/source/api/solvers/"
"idaklu_solver.html>`__ as the default values for the ``rtol`` and ``atol`` "
"arguments to the solver ``__init__`` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:76
msgid "Setting tolerances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:78
msgid ""
"Generally, a smaller value for the tolerances will lead to a more accurate "
"solution, but will also require more computational effort. For examples, "
"here are the timings for the default SPM and DFN models with different "
"tolerances ranging from :math:`10^{-2}` to :math:`10^{-6}`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# time how long it takes to solve the model with different tolerances\n"
"models = [pybamm.lithium_ion.SPM(), pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()]\n"
"model_names = [\"SPM\", \"DFN\"]\n"
"atols = [1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8]\n"
"rtols = [1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8]\n"
"results = np.zeros((len(models), len(atols), len(rtols)))\n"
"for imodel, model in enumerate(models):\n"
"    for iatol, atol in enumerate(atols):\n"
"        for irtol, rtol in enumerate(rtols):\n"
"            solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(atol=atol, rtol=rtol)\n"
"            sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, solver=solver)\n"
"            sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])\n"
"            start_time = time.perf_counter()\n"
"            sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])\n"
"            end_time = time.perf_counter()\n"
"            results[imodel, iatol, irtol] = end_time - start_time\n"
"\n"
"# plot results in a separate plot for each model\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"fig, ax = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(10, 5))\n"
"for imodel, _model in enumerate(models):\n"
"    ax[imodel].imshow(\n"
"        results[imodel],\n"
"        vmin=results[imodel].min(),\n"
"        vmax=results[imodel].max(),\n"
"        cmap=\"viridis\",\n"
"    )\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_xticks(range(len(rtols)))\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_xticklabels([f\"{rtol:.0e}\" for rtol in rtols])\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_xlabel(\"rtol\")\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_yticks(range(len(atols)))\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_yticklabels([f\"{atol:.0e}\" for atol in atols])\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_ylabel(\"atol\")\n"
"    ax[imodel].set_title(model_names[imodel])\n"
"    ax[imodel].text(\n"
"        0,\n"
"        0,\n"
"        f\"{results[imodel].min():.4f} s\",\n"
"        ha=\"center\",\n"
"        va=\"center\",\n"
"        color=\"white\",\n"
"        fontsize=12,\n"
"    )\n"
"    ax[imodel].text(\n"
"        3,\n"
"        3,\n"
"        f\"{results[imodel].max():.4f} s\",\n"
"        ha=\"center\",\n"
"        va=\"center\",\n"
"        color=\"black\",\n"
"        fontsize=12,\n"
"    )\n"
"\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:162
msgid ""
"Thus, the choice of tolerances is a trade-off between accuracy and "
"computational time. It can often be difficult to know what tolerances to "
"use, but a good starting point is to use the default tolerances and then "
"adjust them if the solver is too slow or too inaccurate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:164
msgid ""
"You can also use a convergence study to determine the best tolerances for "
"your problem, using either an analytical solution (if available) or by "
"comparing the solution to a high-tolerance solution. For example, lets "
"compare the solution of a high-tolerance SPM model to a lower-tolerance SPM "
"model while varying the tolerances. We'll pick some goal relative and "
"absolute tolerances of :math:`10^{-4}` and :math:`10^{-6}` respectively and "
"use a similar error norm as the solve. We'll then then vary the tolerances "
"from :math:`10^{-2}` to :math:`10^{-6}` and see when the solution is within "
"the set goal tolerances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"models = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"high_tol = 1e-10\n"
"goal_rtol = 1e-4\n"
"goal_atol = 1e-6\n"
"t_eval = np.linspace(0, 3600, 100)\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(atol=high_tol, rtol=high_tol)\n"
"sim = pybamm.Simulation(models, solver=solver)\n"
"high_tol_sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])[\"Voltage [V]\"](t_eval)\n"
"\n"
"tols = [1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8]\n"
"results = np.zeros(len(tols))\n"
"for itol, tol in enumerate(tols):\n"
"    solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver(atol=tol, rtol=tol)\n"
"    sim = pybamm.Simulation(model, solver=solver)\n"
"    sol = sim.solve([0, 3600])[\"Voltage [V]\"](t_eval)\n"
"    results[itol] = np.sqrt(\n"
"        np.sum(\n"
"            ((sol - high_tol_sol) / (goal_rtol * np.abs(high_tol_sol) + "
"goal_atol)) ** 2\n"
"        )\n"
"        / len(sol)\n"
"    )\n"
"\n"
"# plot results on a log scale\n"
"plt.figure()\n"
"plt.plot(tols, results, \"o-\")\n"
"plt.yscale(\"log\")\n"
"plt.xscale(\"log\")\n"
"plt.xlabel(\"tolerance\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"error norm\")\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/performance/05-solver-tolerances.ipynb:223
msgid ""
"This gives us some justification that a tolerance of :math:`10^{-4}` is a "
"good compromise between accuracy and computational time for this problem. "
"However, the choice of tolerances will be highly problem-dependent, and your "
"measure of error may be different to the one used here, so it is always best "
"to determine the appropriate error measure and convergence study for your "
"particular problem."
msgstr ""
